\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The sign of the discrete Laplacian on functions}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{On functions, the operator of
\citet{pp. 23-24}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec}
is a positive weighted sum of #{f} at the vertex minus #{f} at
the neighbors, under the positive volume convention. Before
the vertex formula, the source defines the smooth operator on
functions by their codifferential followed by the exterior
derivative. Their displayed equation has a square on the left
and #{\delta\extd} on the right. Their #{\delta} in that
equation is the codifferential. In these notes #{\delta} is
only the Kronecker delta. The operator they write as a square
is the positive operator #{- \covsq}, not the square
#{\covsq}. The vertex formula has that sign. The
combination of the exterior derivative with its adjoint,
#{\extd\,\extdag+\extdag\,\extd}, in
\citet{(4.4), p. 94}{dodziuk1976hodge}
is the same positive counterpart for the Whitney inner product
of \citet{Definition 4.1, p. 93}{dodziuk1976hodge}. Dodziuk
writes that adjoint as #{\delta}. That letter is #{\extdag}
for the Whitney inner product, not the Kronecker delta. That
pairing is not the counting product.}

\p{The primal-dual star and the codifferential are stated in
\citet{Definition 4.1.1, pp. 40-41}{hirani2003dec},
\citet{Lemma 4.1.3, p. 42}{hirani2003dec}
and
\citet{Definition 4.2.1, p. 42}{hirani2003dec}.
The double star multiplies a #{p}-form by
#{(-1)^{p(n-p)}}. Hirani's codifferential sends a
#{(p+1)}-form to a #{p}-form by their
#{\delta\beta=(-1)^{np+1}\ast\extd\ast\beta}, so #{p}
is the degree of the value. Their #{\delta} is the
codifferential, and #{\delta} in these notes is only the
Kronecker delta. Their #{\ast} takes primal cochains to dual
cochains. It is not the dual of \ref{fdip-00QQ}, which writes
#{\star A=\tilde A\psI}. At degrees #{0} and #{n} the
star uses an orientation sign,
\citet{Remark 4.1.2, p. 41}{hirani2003dec}. Those signs
stay in the formula.}
